# =============================================================================
# DecisionLedger — Environment Variables
# Copy to .env and fill in real values. Never commit .env.
#
# BREAKING in this revision: Postgres role/db default renamed
# `account_takeover` → `decisionledger`. Update local .env files; recreate
# the Postgres volume (`docker compose down -v`) so the new role initializes.
#
# BREAKING (RAG policy gate moved into gates/policy_rag): ELASTICSEARCH_URL and
# ELASTICSEARCH_PORT are now POLICY_RAG_GATE_ELASTICSEARCH_URL and
# POLICY_RAG_GATE_ELASTICSEARCH_PORT (the old names are ignored), and policy
# chunks moved to the gate's own `policy_rag` schema, which starts empty on an
# existing volume: re-index with `make prepare-reasoners` (or reset with
# `docker compose down -v`), or the gate retrieves on BM25 evidence alone.
#
# Three namespaces:
#   - Unprefixed vars   → framework configuration (Postgres — the only
#                         framework infrastructure — logging, reasoner
#                         composition, the registration file, mandatory
#                         gates, the identity resolver, API keys)
#                         consumed by FrameworkSettings in app/settings.py;
#                         the LLM providers' own key variables, which their
#                         SDKs read directly (the RAG policy gate's settings
#                         read them only to fail fast); and the client-side
#                         DECISIONLEDGER_URL / DECISIONLEDGER_KEY that the
#                         MCP server and the conformance suite read (last
#                         section).
#   - <REASONER>_ vars  → reasoner-specific config consumed by that
#                         reasoner's own Settings class. Today: ATO_ for
#                         the Account Takeover reasoner.
#   - <GATE>_ vars      → gate-specific config consumed by that gate's own
#                         Settings class. Today: RULES_GATE_ for the rules
#                         gate (gates/rules) and POLICY_RAG_GATE_ for the
#                         RAG policy gate (gates/policy_rag).
#
# Reasoners share the framework's Postgres deployment; tenant separation is
# logical (`reasoner_id` row filter), not connection-level. Infrastructure a
# reasoner or gate needs for itself (ATO's Redis feature state, the RAG
# policy gate's Elasticsearch) is configured under that plugin's prefix.
# =============================================================================

# --- LLM credentials (the providers' own variables). OpenAI: the RAG policy
# gate's LLM client and its eval's RAGAS scorer. Anthropic: that eval's
# faithfulness and citation judges. ---
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...

# --- PostgreSQL (the framework's; the RAG policy gate keeps its own schema in
# the same database). docker-compose.yml initializes the database from the
# same variables; unset, both it and FrameworkSettings default to
# decisionledger for the user, the password, and the database. ---
POSTGRES_USER=decisionledger
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me-in-production
POSTGRES_DB=decisionledger
POSTGRES_HOST=localhost
POSTGRES_PORT=5432

# --- Reasoner composition ---
# Comma-separated allowlist of the installed reasoner plugins (by reasoner_id)
# whose registrations the ledger composes; the ledger runs no reasoner code.
# Blank = every plugin installed under the `decisionledger.reasoners`
# entry-point group. It filters plugins only: registrations from
# DECISIONLEDGER_REGISTRATIONS and the ones written over the API
# (PUT /api/v1/reasoners/{reasoner_id}, DR-33) are always served.
DECISIONLEDGER_REASONERS=

# Comma-separated gate_id values the deployment runs on every decision of every
# reasoner, recorded or gated, on top of each registration's own gate set
# (DR-29). Each must be composed, and must accept an empty configuration from
# any reasoner that does not bind it, or startup fails. Blank mandates none.
DECISIONLEDGER_MANDATORY_GATES=

# YAML file of reasoner registrations, composed with the installed reasoner
# plugins' (DR-31): one way a reasoner that runs as its own service, with no
# plugin installed here, registers (the other is PUT /api/v1/reasoners/{id}
# with the operator key, DR-33). A reasoner_id registered by two sources
# refuses startup, and the file's registrations cannot be replaced over the
# API. Example: conformance/registrations.yaml. Blank reads no file.
DECISIONLEDGER_REGISTRATIONS=

# --- Identity (DR-32) ---
# The deployment's one active identity resolver: the name of a plugin installed
# under the decisionledger.identity_resolvers entry-point group (an
# enterprise's crosswalk; the framework ships none). Blank (or passthrough)
# keeps the passthrough default: each decision's subject is the one its
# reasoner names (its entity_id, or the id its registration's declared native
# namespace derives), recorded unresolved. An installed resolver is never
# activated implicitly; a name that is not installed refuses startup. The
# ledger resolves after intake validation and before the gates (50 ms default
# budget); a resolver that does not match, fails, or times out leaves the
# subject unresolved and never refuses the decision.
DECISIONLEDGER_IDENTITY_RESOLVER=

# --- Ledger API keys (DR-31) ---
# BREAKING (Phase 3): every /api/v1 route needs a key in the
# X-DecisionLedger-Key header; with no key configured the API answers 401 to
# everything but /health. Keys live here or in a mounted file only: no user
# model, no sessions, no key issuance.
#
# Per-reasoner keys, comma-separated reasoner_id:key pairs, for example
# example-reasoner:<secret>,ato-reasoner:<secret>. A key writes and reads its
# own reasoner's decisions only. Use long random secrets
# (python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"); blank keeps
# the API closed (401).
DECISIONLEDGER_API_KEYS=
# Optional YAML file mapping reasoner_id to a key or a list of keys (a
# mounted secret), read in addition to DECISIONLEDGER_API_KEYS.
DECISIONLEDGER_API_KEYS_FILE=
# The operator key: one shared secret that reads every reasoner's decisions,
# resolves held ones, and registers reasoners over the API (DR-33; registering
# issues no key: add the new reasoner's key above and restart). Blank
# disables operator access.
DECISIONLEDGER_OPERATOR_KEY=

# --- Logging (process-wide) ---
LOG_JSON=true
LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# =============================================================================
# Rules gate — RULES_GATE_-prefixed
# =============================================================================

# Directory of rule-set YAML files the rules gate registers at startup (one
# rule set per file; see gates/rules/README.md). Blank = no rule sets: every
# decision routed to the rules gate is held for review.
RULES_GATE_RULESETS_DIR=

# =============================================================================
# RAG policy gate — POLICY_RAG_GATE_-prefixed
# =============================================================================

# The gate's Elasticsearch (BM25 leg; one index, chunks tagged by
# reasoner_id). Its dense chunks live in Postgres, in the gate's own
# policy_rag schema, reached with the POSTGRES_* settings above.
POLICY_RAG_GATE_ELASTICSEARCH_URL=http://localhost:9200

# Host port for the elasticsearch service in gates/policy_rag/compose.yaml.
# POLICY_RAG_GATE_ELASTICSEARCH_PORT=9200

# The gate's asset root (DR-33): where a reasoner registered without a plugin
# (by DECISIONLEDGER_REGISTRATIONS or over the API) ships its prompts and
# policy corpus, as <root>/<reasoner_id>/prompts/*.yaml and
# <root>/<reasoner_id>/corpus/**/*.md. The gate reads a reasoner's directory
# when it admits that reasoner's binding; the corpus loader indexes its
# corpus/. Blank = none: the gate serves plugin reasoners only.
POLICY_RAG_GATE_ASSETS_ROOT=

# =============================================================================
# Account Takeover reasoner — ATO_-prefixed
# =============================================================================

# Redis used for ATO's sliding-window feature state and login-event stream.
ATO_REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379

# Host port for the redis service in reasoners/account_takeover/compose.yaml.
# ATO_REDIS_PORT=6379

# Path to the trained XGBoost model artifact. Built by
# `make train-account_takeover` (or `make train`, every reasoner).
# Optional: the default is the artifact shipped inside the reasoner package,
# resolved absolutely, so set this only to point at a different model.
# ATO_SCORER_MODEL_PATH=/abs/path/to/ato-v1.ubj

# Version label of ATO's policy corpus, sent in ATO's RAG policy gate context
# (gate_config.corpus_version) and so recorded in every ATO bundle. Update
# when ingesting a new corpus snapshot via the corpus loader.
ATO_CORPUS_VERSION=ato-v1

# The ledger ATO's own service (make serve-account_takeover) submits to.
# Blank runs the ledger in ATO's process; a URL calls that ledger over HTTP
# with ATO_LEDGER_KEY (one of the ledger's DECISIONLEDGER_API_KEYS).
ATO_LEDGER_URL=
ATO_LEDGER_KEY=

# =============================================================================
# Ledger clients — read by the MCP server (`uv run decisionledger-mcp`,
# integrations/mcp) and the conformance suite (`make conformance`)
# =============================================================================
# The ledger the six MCP tools call (default http://localhost:8000), and the
# key they act with (a reasoner's key, or the operator key for reads only).
# The MCP server also reads DECISIONLEDGER_OPERATOR_KEY, the same name the
# ledger reads above, and sends it on resolve_hold only. The conformance suite
# reads DECISIONLEDGER_URL and DECISIONLEDGER_KEY (the example reasoner's key),
# plus DECISIONLEDGER_OTHER_KEY and DECISIONLEDGER_OPERATOR_KEY (see
# conformance/README.md). Both read the process environment only, not this
# file: export them, or let direnv's dotenv (.envrc) export this file.
# DECISIONLEDGER_URL=http://localhost:8000
# DECISIONLEDGER_KEY=<a reasoner's key from DECISIONLEDGER_API_KEYS>
